"Organizes potential risk sources hierarchically to support systematic identification and coverage reviews.\n\nUse when: Check whether a project risk workshop has missed major source categories. Compare risk exposure themes across related projects.\n\nAvoid when: The main question is a risk's likelihood, impact or response sequence.\n\nVisual grammar: Parent-child branches classify risk sources from broad to specific. Leaf categories guide scenario identification rather than imply events. References connect identified risks to relevant source categories.\n\nBuild: Define the project boundary and choose a suitable source taxonomy. Refine major categories to useful identification prompts. Classify actual risk scenarios and review uncovered or overlapping areas.\n\nCheck: Categories describe sources rather than mixing causes, effects and responses. Peer branches have comparable scope. An empty category means unchecked or no identified risks, as explicitly stated.\n\nPitfalls: Treating the hierarchy as proof every risk has been discovered. Duplicating the same scenario and counting it as separate exposure.\n\nExample: A festival project groups risks under weather, suppliers, staffing and permissions, then uses those branches to guide scenario workshops.\n\nAlternatives: Risk matrix, Work breakdown structure."
